win32u: end the SC_MOVE/SC_SIZE loop when the mouse button is released.

sys_command_size_move() only tests for the end of a drag on messages it
dispatches. An application can consume the WM_LBUTTONUP itself -- a dockable
panel accepting a drop does exactly that -- and a stream of WM_MOUSEMOVE takes
the other path through the loop, so the loop can spin holding the
GUI_INMOVESIZE capture long after the button came up. The layout has already
changed underneath; the UI stays stuck until some unrelated click finally
delivers a message that trips the existing check.

Test the button state every iteration instead, and only for moves that began
with the mouse, so a keyboard move still waits for Return or Escape.

The message that revealed it is dispatched rather than dropped.
NtUserGetMessage has already removed it from the queue, and it is whatever
happened to arrive -- commonly the private message a UI toolkit uses to drain
its dispatcher queue. Losing that leaves the application in a worse state than
the spin being fixed, which is the same reasoning upstream gives for placing
its own button check after the dispatch.

Verified against Affinity 3: dragging a floating panel onto a dock target
releases the capture as the button comes up rather than at the next unrelated
click, and tools/kbmove.c confirms the keyboard path still moves a window and
still exits on Return.

--- a/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
@@ -676,7 +676,7 @@
 static void sys_command_size_move( HWND hwnd, WPARAM wparam )
 {
     DWORD msg_pos = NtUserGetMessagePos();
-    BOOL thickframe, drag_full_windows = TRUE, moved = FALSE;
+    BOOL thickframe, drag_full_windows = TRUE, moved = FALSE, mouse_drag;
     RECT sizing_rect, mouse_rect, orig_rect;
     UINT hittest = wparam & 0x0f;
     UINT syscommand = wparam & 0xfff0;
@@ -779,6 +779,7 @@
 
     send_message( hwnd, WM_ENTERSIZEMOVE, 0, 0 );
     set_capture_window( hwnd, GUI_INMOVESIZE, NULL );
+    mouse_drag = (NtUserGetKeyState( VK_LBUTTON ) & 0x8000) != 0;
 
     for (;;)
     {
@@ -787,6 +788,30 @@
         if (!NtUserGetMessage( &msg, 0, 0, 0 )) break;
         if (NtUserCallMsgFilter( &msg, MSGF_SIZE )) continue;
 
+        /* An application can consume the WM_LBUTTONUP itself: a dockable panel
+         * accepting a drop does exactly that, as does a window that is
+         * re-parented or destroyed as part of the drop. The check further down
+         * only runs for messages we dispatch, and a stream of WM_MOUSEMOVE
+         * takes the other path, so we can spin here holding the
+         * GUI_INMOVESIZE capture long after the button came up -- the layout
+         * has changed underneath but the UI stays stuck until some unrelated
+         * click finally delivers a message that trips that check. Test the
+         * button every iteration instead. Only for moves that began with the
+         * mouse, so keyboard moves still wait for Return or Escape. */
+        if (mouse_drag && !(NtUserGetKeyState( VK_LBUTTON ) & 0x8000))
+        {
+            /* Deliver the message we just took. NtUserGetMessage has already
+             * removed it from the queue, so breaking here drops it -- and it is
+             * whatever happened to arrive, commonly the private message a UI
+             * toolkit uses to drain its dispatcher queue. Losing that leaves
+             * the application in a worse state than the spin this exits for,
+             * which is exactly the reasoning upstream gives for putting its own
+             * button check after the dispatch. */
+            NtUserTranslateMessage( &msg, 0 );
+            NtUserDispatchMessage( &msg );
+            break;
+        }
+
         /* Exit on button-up, Return, or Esc */
         if (msg.message == WM_LBUTTONUP ||
             (msg.message == WM_KEYDOWN && (msg.wParam == VK_RETURN || msg.wParam == VK_ESCAPE)))
